<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maria Kunigunde of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1740">1740</a>–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maria Kunigunde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and elector of Saxony, and sister of Clemens Wenceslaus, the last elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. From 1776 she was princess-abbess of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> and Thorn, the last to hold either office. She lost Thorn when French forces overran it in 1794, and Essen when it was secularised and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1803. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-kunigunde-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Maria Kunigunde of Saxony" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pietro Rotari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kunigunde_by_Rotari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
